    "apiVersionNote": "The response signature reports version 1.2, but the API documentation page header reads \"Version: 1.3 (2025 June)\" (changelog for 1.3: \"Update to mention TIME_TYPE setting\").",
    "quantity": 31,
    "quantityLabels": [
      "ObsEcLon",
      "ObsEcLat"
    ],
    "frameDescription": "Observer-centered IAU76/80 ecliptic-of-date longitude and latitude of the target centers' apparent position with light-time, gravitational deflection of light, stellar aberration, and optional atmospheric refraction correction.  For non-Earth sites, the Earth ecliptic at reference-frame standard epoch is used (J2000 or B1950).",
    "frameDescriptionSource": "https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/manual.html#observer-ecliptic-longitude--latitude (Horizons manual, observer quantity 31)",
    "frameDescriptionInResponse": "Observer-centered IAU76/80 ecliptic-of-date longitude and latitude of the target centers' apparent position, with light-time, gravitational deflection of light, and stellar aberrations.  Units: DEGREES",
    "eclipticOfDateDefinition": "Used for some observer table quantities, this dynamic frame uses the IAU76/80 time-varying Earth obliquity model to construct the ecliptic x-y plane of-date over modern eras and the long-term IAU76/80-compatible model of Owen, a long-term integration of Kinoshita’s polar equations of motion, for times more than 200 years from the year 2000. (https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/manual.html#ecliptic-of-date)",
    "equinoxOfDate": "TRUE equinox of date (nutation in longitude included). The documentation does not say mean or true explicitly; this was determined empirically, see frameCheck.",
    "frameCheck": {
      "method": "Queried quantities 2 (apparent RA/DEC with respect to the Earth true equator and equinox of date, compensated for \"precession & nutation\") and 31 together for Sun, Moon and Venus at the same 5 instants. Rotated q2 into ecliptic coordinates about the true equinox using the true obliquity (IAU1976 mean obliquity + IAU1980 nutation in obliquity, 40-term series) and compared the result with q31.",
      "result": "q31 matches the true-equinox, true-obliquity transform of q2 to <=0.0015 arcsec in longitude and <=0.009 arcsec in latitude for all 15 rows. A mean-equinox reading would differ by -dpsi (about -13.7, -13.3 and +13.9 arcsec on 1975-12-31, 1990-10-15 and 2000-01-01). A mean-obliquity transform leaves latitude residuals up to 8.7 arcsec. Conclusion: ObsEcLon/ObsEcLat is the geocentric apparent position referred to the ecliptic of date and the TRUE equinox of date (the standard \"apparent longitude\").",
      "implicationForSidereal": "Subtract a nutation-inclusive (true) ayanamsa from these longitudes, or compare mean-equinox longitudes against a mean ayanamsa.",
      "rawFiles": [
        "framecheck_sun_10_q2_q31.txt",
        "framecheck_moon_301_q2_q31.txt",
        "framecheck_venus_299_q2_q31.txt"
      ],
      "independentCheck": {
        "source": "IMCCE - Observatoire de Paris, https://ftp.imcce.fr/pub/ephem/sun/seasons/ (files 1950-99 and 2000-49; README: \"Solstices and Equinoxes between 1900 and 2999 in dynamical time.\" and \"The apparent geocentric longitude of the Sun is equal to 0 degree for the Spring equinox and equal to 180 degrees for the Automn equinox.\" Computed with a truncated VSOP87.)",
        "method": "Queried Horizons q31 for the Sun at the IMCCE event times (TIME_TYPE=TT). The IMCCE times are rounded to 1 minute, which allows about +/-1.3 arcsec. A mean-equinox q31 would be off by -dpsi, i.e. about -13.5, -13.6, +14.7 and +16.8 arcsec at the 1975, 1990, 2000-03 and 2000-12 events (IAU1980 nutation). The 2025 event does not discriminate (dpsi is about +0.7 arcsec).",
        "rawFile": "crosscheck_sun_10_q31_at_imcce_seasons_TT.txt",
        "rows": [
          {
            "imcceInstant": "1975-12-22 11:46 TT",
            "event": "winter solstice",
            "expectedApparentLonDeg": 270,
            "horizonsJdTT": 2442768.990277778,
            "horizonsObsEcLonDeg": 269.99976,
            "differenceArcsec": -0.86
          },
          {
            "imcceInstant": "1990-09-23 06:56 TT",
            "event": "autumn equinox",
            "expectedApparentLonDeg": 180,
            "horizonsJdTT": 2448157.788888889,
            "horizonsObsEcLonDeg": 179.9996813,
            "differenceArcsec": -1.15
          },
          {
            "imcceInstant": "2000-03-20 07:36 TT",
            "event": "spring equinox",
            "expectedApparentLonDeg": 0,
            "horizonsJdTT": 2451623.816666667,
            "horizonsObsEcLonDeg": 359.9997735,
            "differenceArcsec": -0.82
          },
          {
            "imcceInstant": "2000-12-21 13:39 TT",
            "event": "winter solstice",
            "expectedApparentLonDeg": 270,
            "horizonsJdTT": 2451900.06875,
            "horizonsObsEcLonDeg": 270.0003443,
            "differenceArcsec": 1.24
          },
          {
            "imcceInstant": "2025-03-20 09:03 TT",
            "event": "spring equinox",
            "expectedApparentLonDeg": 0,
            "horizonsJdTT": 2460754.877083333,
            "horizonsObsEcLonDeg": 0.0002387,
            "differenceArcsec": 0.86
          }
        ],
        "result": "All differences are within the +/-1.3 arcsec rounding window, which is consistent with q31 being the apparent longitude referred to the true equinox of date."
      }
    },
